Mechanical source migrations
============================

One transform per script. Each takes a tree root, reports by default, and
writes only with --apply:

    python3 Scripts/Migration/<script>.py Compiler
    python3 Scripts/Migration/<script>.py Compiler --apply
    python3 Scripts/Migration/<script>.py Compiler --apply --only Lexer

These run on a COPY of the tree, not on Compiler/ in place. Compiler/ stays
Stage 0 syntax because Stage 0 is what compiles it; the Stage 1 test corpus is
the migrated copy. private/portdry/ was the first one.

migrate.py is the pass manager: it threads every file through all the
passes below in order, in memory, and writes once. --list prints the order
and why each pass sits where it does; --passes / --skip / --from / --to pick
a slice; --check-idempotent re-runs the pipeline on its own output;
--selftest runs each pass's own table. It refuses --apply on Compiler/, Lib/
or Linker/ themselves unless given --in-place.

    python3 Scripts/Migration/migrate.py private/portdry --check-idempotent
    python3 Scripts/Migration/migrate.py private/portdry --apply

kairo_lex.py is the shared lexer-lite and CLI driver. It is not a parser: it
only has to be right about which byte offsets are Kairo CODE, so a rewrite
never fires inside a // or /* */ comment, a string literal, or an
inline "c++" body. f-string {...} interiors ARE code and are rewritten.
Compiler/Native, Lib/bootstrap and Lib/llvm-runtimes are skipped -- C++ by
design.


v0.0.1 -> v0.1.0 port
---------------------

Split out of private/port_dryrun.py, which carried the first six of these plus the
lexer in one file. Run order, which is the order the dry-run used:

    ternary_to_if.py        A if C else B  ->  if C { A } else { B }
    switch_to_match.py      switch -> match + colon labels -> braced arms (idempotent;
                            also fixes arms inside an already-renamed match)
    stub_ffi_imports.py     ffi "c++" import -> commented out   (DRY-RUN ONLY)
    drop_turbofish.py       Foo::<T> -> Foo<T>
    case_labels_to_arms.py  case A: case B: -> case A, B { ... }  (superseded by
                            switch_to_match.py; a no-op after it)
    ranged_for_binding.py   for x: T in xs -> for var x: T in xs
    qualified_var_binding.py  static/const/eval var x -> static/const/eval x
    defaulted_fn_suffix.py  default fn X(self); -> fn X(self) = default  (and delete)
    split_oneliner_blocks.py  if c { a; b; } -> one statement per line  (--all: single-stmt too)
    visibility_first.py     inline pub fn -> pub inline fn  (pub/priv/prot lead the modifiers)
    redundant_parens.py     if (x > 0) { -> if x > 0 {; (a * b) + c -> a * b + c
                            (STAGE 0 SAFE by default; see below)

Two deliberate differences from port_dryrun.py: it hardcoded
private/portdry/ and wrote unconditionally, because it ran on a throwaway copy
of the tree. These take the root as an argument and require --apply.

stub_ffi_imports.py is a dry-run aid, not a port step. It existed so a known
Stage 1 parse gap could not swamp the residual parse-error list. Every stub it
leaves is grep-able (rg PORTDRY-FFI-STUB) and has to come back before the tree
means anything. Do not run it on a tree you intend to keep.

ternary_to_if.py is the one that can get it wrong: it finds where operand A
starts by scanning left with no parser. It reports what it could not parse
rather than guessing, and it will not cross a newline -- in a
newline-terminated language `foo()\nif x {` is a statement, not a ternary, and
only unbalanced brackets tell the two apart. Those sites are by hand:

    rg '^\s+else\s+[^{]*$'


redundant_parens.py -- STAGE 0 SAFE BY DEFAULT
----------------------------------------------

Two rules. Rule 1: a pair wrapping the WHOLE head of if / else if / while /
match / switch / for, i.e. whose `)` is followed by the body `{`, goes
(`if (x > 0) {` -> `if x > 0 {`; `for` only in its C-style three-clause form).
Rule 2: anywhere else in code, a pair whose interior parses as one expression
and that precedence/associativity make redundant goes; a needed one stays
(`a - (b - c)`, `(a + b) * c`). Each removal is decided against the text left
by the ones before it, outer first, so `((a + b)) * c` ends as `(a + b) * c`.
`if (a) && (b) {` is NOT a whole-head wrap, so rule 1 leaves it -- but both
pairs hold one operand, so rule 2 still makes it `if a && b {`. Intended.

The table is ExprParse.k's. Where Spec/Docs/operators.md disagrees with the
parser -- `<=>`, `in`, `.*` / `->*` / `?.*` / `?->*`, range associativity,
`??` -- the operator is treated as uncertain: a pair whose fate depends on its
precedence against another operator is kept, in both modes.

Default mode is Stage 0 safe, because Compiler/ is compiled by Stage 0 and
Stage 0's prefix operators swallow the rest of their level. Measured with
`kairo --emit-ir` (2026-10-01): `!a && b` -> `!(a && b)`, `-6 - x` ->
`-(6 - x)`, and `!(a) && b` -> `!((a) && b)` too -- a paren right after the
operator does NOT bound it (bad_pred_fix.py's docstring says otherwise; it is
wrong for this Stage 0). `-x as u8` -> `-(x as u8)`; `x as u8 + 1` is a Stage 0
syntax error. So in safe mode:

    (!a) && b, (*p) += x, (-a) == 0, (-x) as u8   kept
    (x as u8) + 1, (a ^^ b) ^^ c                  kept (as, ^^, === uncertain)
    !(a) && b                                     kept, and reported
    (!a == b) && c                                kept, and reported: Stage 0
                                                  already reads !(a == b)

--no-stage0-safe gives the pure Stage 1 result (`if !a == b && c {`). Only
use it on a tree Stage 0 will never compile. The flag is read from argv, so
migrate.py forwards it.

Reported in both modes: "STAGE 0 DIVERGES" -- a prefix operator followed by a
binary operator inside one level, where the two compilers already read the
source differently. These are real bugs when the file is Stage 0-compiled;
on 2026-10-01 the tree had two, Lib/std/rt/Float8.k:139 and :251
(`(-6 - unb) as u32`, which Stage 0 computes as `-(6 - unb)`).

Never touched: call / index / generic / signature / closure-param parens,
tuples, `({ ... })`, any interior with a top-level `{` (parens reset
NoStructLiteral), a pair followed by `(`, lone `(u8)`, `(5).x`, case
patterns, types (after `:`, `->`, `as`, inside `<...>`), macro bodies and
`name!(...)` / `@attr(...)` arguments, expression-if / Stage 0 ternary
interiors, f-string interpolations (opaque here, unlike kairo_lex), a pair
spanning lines unless a surviving ( or [ encloses it, and any removal that
would glue two operator characters (`-(-a)`). An interior the parser does not
understand is kept and counted (--log-bail lists them). Only paren characters
and the padding they held are deleted; `if(x){` becomes `if x {`.

    python3 Scripts/Migration/redundant_parens.py --selftest
    python3 Scripts/Migration/migrate.py <copy> --passes redundant_parens --check-idempotent

On Compiler/ + Lib/ (2026-10-01, on a copy): 4897 pairs in 178 of 263 files
safe, 5243 in 182 with --no-stage0-safe; idempotent; only paren characters
and padding change. Stage 1 `--type-check-only --print-sema`, before vs after,
columns normalized: identical for every syntax-clean changed file, and for
files Stage 1 already rejects, identical up to the first syntax error (past
it, error recovery drifts with the token count). That check is what found
`x as usize < n` -- the type parser takes `<` as generic args -- so a cast
keeps its parens before a `<`-family operator.


inline C++ retirement
---------------------

320 sites in Compiler/: 98 `inline "c++"` blocks (642 body lines) and 222
`__inline_cpp("...")`. They exist because Stage 0's syntax had gaps that were
worked around rather than fixed, so every one of them has a Stage 1 spelling --
the corpus is not complete until they are gone.

The __inline_cpp half, by shape:

    C cast of a literal     141   63.5%    (size_t)5, (uint64_t)0, (u8*)""
    other expr               24   10.8%
    libcxx/std call          22    9.9%
    multi-line body          19    8.6%
    libc call ::f(...)        7    3.2%
    stmt (assign/return)      4    1.8%
    C cast of an expr         3    1.4%
    intrinsic tmpl / decl     2    0.9%

inline_cpp_casts.py       the 141, plus the 3 cast-of-expression. Eight
                          distinct target types across all of them.
                          T* -> *T with the star count carried; a cast to a
                          pointer REQUIRES `unsafe` (`f as unsafe *void`, never
                          `f as *void`); T& has no Stage 1 spelling and is
                          reported, never rewritten. `char` and `wchar_t` are
                          refused rather than guessed. --selftest runs the
                          table without touching a tree.

Still to write: libc calls -> ffi "c" import plus a direct call (7), and the
libcxx/std calls, which libcxx_to_cxx_std.py already handles outside inline
blocks. The 19 multi-line bodies are not expressions -- they are C++ member
declarations spliced into a class (ArenaAllocator.k:159 is a variadic template
member, TokenBuffer.k:63 a move constructor). Those are by hand, and they are
the sites that exercise Stage 1 generics hardest, so they are worth writing
carefully anyway.

One sequencing note. The corpus only means anything if Stage 1 accepts it, and
"Stage 1 accepts it" is the thing under test -- so a bad rewrite and a Stage 1
bug look identical. That is why this is one script per rule and not one porter:
when Stage 1 chokes on file 40 you need to know which rule to suspect, and you
need to re-run one rule against a fresh copy.


Other
-----

hex_escapes_to_chars.py   \x27 -> ' and \x22 -> \" in every string, \x7B /
                          \x7D -> { / } outside f-strings. Stage 0 lexer
                          workarounds (CLAUDE.md); Stage 1 needs none of them.
                          Stage 0's r"" keeps C escapes and Stage 1's is truly
                          raw, so an r"" holding a backslash becomes the plain
                          string it always meant. Comments, char literals and
                          inline "c++" bodies are left alone. --selftest.
                          On Compiler/ (2026-09-28, run on a copy): 293 sites
                          in 45 files; the 20 escapes it leaves are all in
                          comments or char literals.

template_alias_macros.py  __inline_cpp("#define B_T B<T>") -> B<T> at every
                          use; the #define and any #undef block are removed.
                          Only object-like `Name<...>` bodies; any other
                          #define (`sink`, function-like) is reported, never
                          substituted. --selftest.

ref_and_mref.py           ref!(T) / mref(T) in parameter position
                          -> @inout x: T / @move x: T

libcxx_to_cxx_std.py      libcxx::X -> cxx::std::X. Stage 1.5 work, not yet
                          run. `libcxx` is a Stage 0 bootstrap alias
                          (namespace libcxx = ::std, config.hh:53) with
                          nothing behind it in Stage 1 resolution; Stage 1
                          reaches C++'s std through the `cxx` overlay
                          (Compiler/Sema/IMPORTS.md 5).

                          The rename is the easy half. `cxx` binds only in a
                          TU with an ffi import, and cxx::std::X only resolves
                          if X's header is in that TU's closure, so the script
                          reports per file which ffi imports are missing and
                          refuses to guess them. Sites inside inline "c++"
                          blocks are reported MANUAL and never rewritten --
                          cxx::std is not valid C++ there.
